{"stored_at": 1785952004.835357, "payload": {"status": 200, "text": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?><rss version=\"2.0\"\n\txmlns:content=\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\"\n\txmlns:wfw=\"http://wellformedweb.org/CommentAPI/\"\n\txmlns:dc=\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\"\n\txmlns:atom=\"http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom\"\n\txmlns:sy=\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/syndication/\"\n\txmlns:slash=\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/\"\n\t>\n\n<channel>\n\t<title>Artforum</title>\n\t<atom:link href=\"https://www.artforum.com/feed/\" rel=\"self\" type=\"application/rss+xml\" />\n\t<link>https://www.artforum.com</link>\n\t<description></description>\n\t<lastBuildDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 17:13:01 +0000</lastBuildDate>\n\t<language>en-US</language>\n\t<sy:updatePeriod>\n\thourly\t</sy:updatePeriod>\n\t<sy:updateFrequency>\n\t1\t</sy:updateFrequency>\n\t<generator>https://wordpress.org/?v=6.9.5</generator>\n\n<image>\n\t<url>https://www.artforum.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/cropped-AF-Favicon-3.png?w=32</url>\n\t<title>Artforum</title>\n\t<link>https://www.artforum.com</link>\n\t<width>32</width>\n\t<height>32</height>\n</image> \n<site xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">217369877</site>\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Bridgemaker Prize Awarded to San Diego Museum of Art and the Westmoreland Museum of American Art</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/bridgemaker-prize-2026-1234756381/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Thomas Patier]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 17:02:06 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Art Bridges Foundation]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Prizes]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[San Diego Museum of Art]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Westmoreland Museum of American Art]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756381</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Earlier this week, the Art Bridges Foundation announced that two museums—The San Diego Museum of Art and The Westmoreland Museum of American Art—are the recipients of the second annual Bridgemaker Prize. The prize, which this year comes with a $75,000 award to support future projects, is given to Art Bridges’ museum partners whose institutional endeavors [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756381</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Valerie Hillings Appointed Inaugural Director of Guggenheim Abu Dhabi</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/valerie-hillings-appointed-director-of-guggenheim-abu-dhabi-1234756359/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Polly Watson]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 16:11:53 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Appointments]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Guggenheim Abu Dhabi]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Valerie Hillings]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756359</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[The Guggenheim Abu Dhabi, which recently announced that it will open December 11, has named Valerie Hillings as its inaugural director. Hillings joins the institution from Raleigh’s North Carolina Museum of Art (NCMAA), where she has served as director since 2018. Her appointment marks her return to Guggenheim Abu Dhabi, where she was curator and [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756359</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>From Medusa to Nvidia: On the Algorithm’s Primal Scene</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/features/jan-tumlir-jeffrey-stuker-algorithm-1234756309/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Samuel Wilken]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Wed, 05 Aug 2026 14:47:08 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[Features]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[jan tumlir]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[jeffrey stuker]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[myth]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[TECHNOLOGY]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756309</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Tracing the “godly, biological, and machinic” origins of contemporary computation<br>]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756309</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Calls To Rebuild The Glasgow School Of Art’s Mackintosh Building Gain Momentum</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/calls-to-rebuild-the-mackintosh-building-gain-momentum-1234756352/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Theo Belci]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 21:18:27 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Glasgow School of Art]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Mackintosh Building]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756352</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[A petition launched by Scottish architect Ruairidh Moir to rebuild Charles Rennie Mackintosh’s famed Glasgow School of Art building has garnered almost 9,000 signatures. The campaign is a response to years of perceived inaction on the part of the Glasgow School of Art (GSA), with the building still standing unrestored following two fires in 2014 [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756352</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Vindicated Collector Bernardo Paz is Developing a New Museum in Brazil</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/collector-bernardo-paz-developing-new-museum-in-brazil-1234756249/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mika Lee]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 18:23:59 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Bernardo Paz]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Inhotim Institute]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[museums]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756249</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Multimillionaire Brazilian art collector Bernardo Paz, the founder of museum and sculpture garden Instituto Inhotim, is developing a new museum and accompanying botanical garden that will sit directly next to Inhotim, according to reports. Paz is currently leading and funding the new, as yet unnamed museum project, which will eventually function as a nonprofit.  Like [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756249</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Trump Administration’s Assault on Smithsonian Continues With Proposed Budget Cuts</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/trump-assaults-smithsonian-with-proposed-budget-cuts-1234756246/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mika Lee]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 17:38:27 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Smithsonian]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Trump Administration]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756246</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[For the fiscal year 2027, the federal government has proposed lowering the Smithsonian Institution&#8216;s budget to $961.3 million, the lowest prospective funding level since 2020. The submitted budget represents an 11% cut from the $1.08 billion allocated to the Smithsonian in 2026.  This move is just the latest in a long series of deliberate attempts [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756246</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Michelle Grabner Gifts Huge Selection To The John Michael Kohler Arts Center</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/michelle-grabner-gifts-the-john-michael-kohler-arts-center-1234756253/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mika Lee]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 16:57:52 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Gifts]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[John Michael Kohler Arts Center]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Michelle Grabner]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756253</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Artist and curator Michelle Grabner has donated over 2000 pieces of artwork to the John Michael Kohler Arts Center (JMKAC) in Sheboygan, Wisconsin. The gift makes JMKAC the single largest holder of Grabner’s works in the world.  The donated works span across Grabner’s career beginning in the 1990s and consist of 1200 prints and photographs, [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756253</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Francisco Correa Cordero, Founder of New York’s Lubov Gallery, Dead at 37</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/lubov-gallery-founder-francisco-correa-cordero-dead-at-37-1234756263/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Polly Watson]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 15:59:13 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Francisco Correa Cordero]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Lubov]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[OBITUARIES]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756263</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Francisco Correa Cordero, whose ten-year-old Lubov gallery was a beacon for emerging artists, died on August 2 in a Manhattan hospital. He was thirty-seven. His death was announced by his gallery in an Instagram post, which noted that Cordero “passed away peacefully surrounded by what he loved the most—his chosen family, laughter and so much [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756263</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Inaugural Art Week NYC Announces Participating Galleries</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/art-week-nyc-announces-participating-galleries-for-event-1234756235/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Theo Belci]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Art Week NYC]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[New York]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756235</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Today,&#160;Art Week NYC&#160;announced&#160;the participating galleries for its inaugural&#160;citywide event taking place November 11–14, 2026.&#160;The gallery program, presented by Artforum,&#160;is the cornerstone of the inaugural event, bringing together more than 76&#160;world renowned galleries&#160;shaping the&#160;contemporary art world.&#160; Participating galleries include Hauser &#38; Wirth, Gagosian, David Zwirner,&#160;Pace&#160;Gallery, Lisson, Paula Cooper Gallery, and many others across New York’s&#160;gallery districts, [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756235</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Union Members at Royal Museums Greenwich and Other London Institutions Elect to Strike</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/union-members-at-royal-museums-greenwich-elect-to-strike-1234756190/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Theo Belci]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 20:29:07 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[London]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Royal Museums Greenwich]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756190</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Staff at Royal Museums Greenwich (RMG) in UK-based trade union Prospect have decided to strike on August 11 and 12, as per a recent statement. Over 90% of members voted on action, with nearly 77% of those favoring strike action. Operating out of Greenwich’s UNESCO World Heritage site, RMG represents four cultural institutions in London: [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756190</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Jeju Museum Of Art Reveals Final Artist Lineup For 2026 Jeju Biennale</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/jeju-museum-of-art-artist-lineup-for-2026-jeju-biennale-1234756222/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mika Lee]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 19:49:46 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Jeju Biennale]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756222</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[The Jeju Museum of Art has revealed the list of sixty-nine participating artists and collectives for the 2026 Jeju Biennale in a press release. The event, which is set to open on August 25, will run until November 15, and is titled “Iyahong: The Art of Metamorphosis.”  Now in its fifth edition, the Jeju Biennale will take place [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756222</post-id>\t</item>\n\t\t<item>\n\t\t<title>Palais de Tokyo to Close for Renovations in 2027, Sparking Ire with New Temporary Digs</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.artforum.com/news/palais-de-tokyo-to-close-for-renovations-in-2027-1234756211/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Polly Watson]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 18:32:14 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Capital Projects]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[closures]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[La Générale]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Palais de Tokyo]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[POLITICS]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.artforum.com/?p=1234756211</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[Paris’s Palais de Tokyo will close for repairs in July 2027, necessitating a temporary move to a location that turns out to be hotly contested, French daily Libération reports. The noncollecting institution, which since its 2002 founding has been housed in a concrete structure built for the 1937 World’s Fair, recently revitalized its building and [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t<post-id xmlns=\"com-wordpress:feed-additions:1\">1234756211</post-id>\t</item>\n\t</channel>\n</rss>\n", "content_type": "application/rss+xml; charset=UTF-8"}}